      "math_academy_id": "e-4461",
      "problem": "The graph below shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184158/assets/6298e9ee004e642d05d065d1fc2fd325f07d313ac736ef15b190c1b9c90d1019.png)",
      "worked_solution": "To find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184158/assets/d573d9aad231622b2e5dc42a8672d973e5fda518d75894f3bafb0b1a97532d24.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\dfrac{3}{4}.$",
